const year = new Date().getFullYear();

// The home page runs its map down behind the drawing; every other page
// gets the header's sky there instead. See THE SKY in the note above.
const onMap = Astro.url.pathname === '/';

// THE GROUND PROFILE, in a 1440 x 180 box that is stretched to the page's
// width. Every path below is built from these pieces, so they can never
// drift apart. From west to east:
//
//   PLATEAU  the level plain, with the playa's shallow dip
//   RIM      the soft cover sands rounding back from the edge, then the
//            caprock's own cliff: straight down, and cut back under
//   SLOPE    steep, in the sands, flattening at its foot where what
//            has fallen from above lies banked against the ledge
//   BENCH    the top of the sandstone ledge: a slab's edge part way
//            along, a rounded lip, a face with a bedding-plane notch
//            in it, and an undercut at its foot
//   POOL     the hollow that falling water has worn under the lip: a
//            back wall under the ledge, a floor, a low outer rim
//   SKIRT    the red beds below that, steep and then flattening
//   PLAINS   the Rolling Plains: low swells, and one shallow draw with
//            a creek's channel cut in its floor
//
// THE LEDGE IS FITTED TO THE PAINTING. Its top is where the sands rest
// on the sandstone in the picture (about 96) and its face is as thick
// as the picture's sandstone (down to about 103), so the land steps
// exactly where the rock changes. The rim is fitted the same way: the
// cover sands are 2.3 units thick in the picture and the caliche ends
// 6.5 below the plain. If the pictures are ever painted again with
// those beds at other levels, RIM and BENCH have to move with them.
